{"records":[{"id":"g3xxjdhwkt","title":"UBE2M is a stress-inducible dual E2 for neddylation and ubiquitylation that promotes targeted degradation of UBE2F. Weihua Zhou et al.","description":"UBE2M and UBE2F are two family members of neddylation E2 conjugating enzyme that, together with E3s, activate CRLs (Cullin-RING Ligases) by catalyzing cullin neddylation. However, whether and how two E2s cross-talk with each other are largely unknown. Here, we report that UBE2M is a stress-inducible gene subjected to cis-transactivation by HIF-1α and AP1, and MLN4924, a small molecule inhibitor of E1 NEDD8-activating enzyme (NAE), up-regulates UBE2M via blocking degradation of HIF-1α and AP1. UBE2M is a dual E2 for targeted ubiquitylation and degradation of UBE2F, acting as a neddylation E2 to activate CUL3-Keap1 E3 under physiological condition, but as an ubiquitylation E2 for Parkin-DJ-1 E3 under stressed conditions. UBE2M-induced UBE2F degradation leads to CRL5 inactivation and subsequent NOXA accumulation to suppress the growth of lung cancer cells.  Collectively, our study establishes a negative regulatory axis between two neddylation E2s with UBE2M ubiquitylating UBE2F, and two CRLs with CRL3 inactivating CRL5.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-06-06T18:11:47.193Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g3xxjdhwkt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g3xxjdhwkt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yi","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Weihua","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jie","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mingjia","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jinfang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haomin","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hua","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenyi","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ubiquitylation","Lung Carcinoma"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"wynmtr788p","title":"Reconstitution of the destruction complex defines roles of AXIN polymers and APC in beta-catenin capture, phosphorylation, and ubiquitylation","description":"Unprocessed, original data for Ranes et al., 2021\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.molcel.2021.07.013\n\nReconstitution of the destruction complex defines roles of AXIN polymers and APC in beta-catenin capture, phosphorylation, and ubiquitylation\nMichael Ranes, Mariola Zaleska, Saira Sakalas, Ruth Knight, Sebastian Guettler\nDivisions of Structural Biology and Cancer Biology, The Institute of Cancer Research (ICR), London, UK\n\nSummary:\nThe Wnt/beta-catenin pathway is a highly conserved, frequently mutated developmental and cancer pathway. Its output is defined mainly by beta-catenin’s phosphorylation- and ubiquitylation-dependent proteasomal degradation, initiated by the multi-protein beta-catenin destruction complex. The precise mechanisms underlying destruction complex function have remained unknown, largely because of the lack of suitable in vitro systems. Here we describe the in vitro reconstitution of an active human beta-catenin destruction complex from purified components, recapitulating complex assembly, beta-catenin modification, and degradation. We reveal that AXIN1 polymerisation and APC promote beta-catenin capture, phosphorylation, and ubiquitylation. APC facilitates beta-catenin’s flux through the complex by limiting ubiquitylation processivity and directly interacts with the SCF beta-TrCP E3 ligase complex in a beta-TrCP-dependent manner. Oncogenic APC truncation variants, although part of the complex, are functionally impaired. Nonetheless, even the most severely truncated APC variant promotes beta-catenin recruitment, potentially explaining why APC loss is selected against during carcinogenesis. These findings exemplify the power of biochemical reconstitution to interrogate the molecular mechanisms of Wnt/beta-catenin signalling.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-05T14:18:30.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wynmtr788p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wynmtr788p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Ranes","orcid":"0000-0003-2531-4850"},{"first_name":"Mariola","last_name":"Zaleska","orcid":"0000-0002-3946-4254"},{"first_name":"Saira","last_name":"Sakalas","orcid":"0000-0002-7922-5119"},{"first_name":"Ruth","last_name":"Knight","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sebastian","last_name":"Guettler","orcid":"0000-0002-3135-1546"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Enzymology","Cell Signaling","Phosphorylation","Oncogene Protein","Colorectal Cancer","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wbhz86pjs5","title":"A CK2–β-TRCP kinase–E3 ubiquitin ligase cascade is a metabolic sensor regulating Tryptophan 2,3-dioxygenase stability","description":"Small molecules toggling the ubiquitin-proteasome system (UPS) are powerful regulators of protein degradation, yet how endogenous ligands gate UPS decisions remains poorly understood. Here we define control of UPS access to Tryptophan-2,3-dioxygenase (TDO2), which converts the essential amino acid tryptophan (Trp) to N-formylkynurenine. When Trp concentrations are limiting, TDO2 is degraded to avert tryptophanemia. We discovered that a CK2–β-TrCP kinase-E3 ligase cascade generates and recognizes phosphodegrons in TDO2 unless shielded by Trp binding an exosite distal from the catalytic site. Effects of Trp analogs on CK2–β-TrCP-dependent ubiquitylation indicated the indole, amino, and carboxylate groups collectively protect TDO2. Cryo-EM reveals these moieties organize a Trp-ordered flap that covers the exosite and thereby restricts generation of adjacent phosphodegrons. In the absence of Trp, the flap is flexible, enabling phosphorylation-coupled ubiquitylation. Overall, our data uncovered an endogenous small molecule conformationally stabilizing its own metabolizing enzyme through protection from a phosphorylation-ubiquitylation cascade.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-12T16:28:29.28Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wbhz86pjs5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wbhz86pjs5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alina","last_name":"Thielen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bastian","last_name":"Bräuning","orcid":"0000-0002-7194-2500"},{"first_name":"Karthik","last_name":"Gottemukkala","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Judith","last_name":"Müller","orcid":null},{"first_name":"J. Rajan","last_name":"Prabu","orcid":"0000-0002-7726-9310"},{"first_name":"Pooja","last_name":"Verma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Simon","last_name":"Klaessens","orcid":"0000-0003-2285-9177"},{"first_name":"Benoit","last_name":"Van den Eynde","orcid":"0000-0002-4995-3270"},{"first_name":"Gary","last_name":"Kleiger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Murray","orcid":"0000-0001-6329-9802"},{"first_name":"Brenda","last_name":"Schulman","orcid":"0000-0002-3083-1126"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","Metabolism","Ubiquitin Ligase"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tvh5nhwx3c","title":"Cullin-RING E3 ubiquitin ligase 4 regulates neurite morphogenesis during neurodevelopment. Tammy et al.","description":"LC-MS/MS raw data for identification of Immunoprecipitated proteins by Cul4a and Cul4b","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-02T14:30:43.614Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tvh5nhwx3c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tvh5nhwx3c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bongki","last_name":"Cho","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biomolecule Interaction"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9xwvvvcsz6","title":"Glucose-induced CRL4COP1-p53 degradation axis amplifies glycometabolism to drive tumorigenesis ","description":"The diabetes-cancer association remains under-explained. Here, we describe a glucose signaling axis that reinforces glucose uptake and glycolysis to consolidate the Warburg effect and overcome tumor suppression. Specifically, glucose-dependent CK2 O-GlcNAcylation impedes its phosphorylation of CSN2, a modification required for the deneddylase CSN to sequester Cullin RING Ligase 4 (CRL4). Glucose therefore elicits CSN-CRL4 dissociation to assemble the CRL4^COP1 E3 ligase, which targets p53 to derepress glycolytic enzymes. Genetic or pharmacologic disruption of the O-GlcNAc-CK2-CSN2-CRL4^COP1 axis abrogates glucose-induced p53 degradation and cancer cell proliferation. Diet-induced overnutrition up-regulates the CRL4^COP1-p53 axis to promote PyMT-induced mammary tumorigenesis in wildtype but not mammary gland-specific p53 knockout mice. These effects of overnutrition are reversed by P28, an investigational peptide inhibitor of COP1-p53 interaction. Thus, glycometabolism self-amplifies via a glucose-induced post-translational modification cascade culminating in CRL4^COP1-mediated p53 degradation. Such mutation-independent p53 checkpoint bypass may represent the carcinogenic origin and targetable vulnerability of hyperglycemia-driven cancer. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:19:12.339Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9xwvvvcsz6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9xwvvvcsz6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Feng","last_name":"Rao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Su","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yifan","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gtwgyd5gzs","title":"LINF_210005300","description":"RING-box E3 ubiquitin ligase; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-06-25T00:12:47.679Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gtwgyd5gzs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gtwgyd5gzs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nyyjd6k7dm","title":"LINF_240005600","description":"RING-type E3 ligase 3; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-25T15:37:24.917Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nyyjd6k7dm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nyyjd6k7dm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z57k4xyh35","title":"LINF_240019200","description":"RING-type E3 ligase 1; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-01T12:18:37.836Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z57k4xyh35","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z57k4xyh35"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"c6727gf73g","title":"LINF_180017000","description":"RING-type E3 ligase 2; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-28T17:15:20.692Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/c6727gf73g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/c6727gf73g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v28kxyw6vn","title":"LINF_240019300","description":"RING-type E3 ligase 4; Leishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-01T12:18:41.38Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v28kxyw6vn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v28kxyw6vn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":133258,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":155,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":133258,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132692,"restricted":64,"embargoed":502},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2228},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1444},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":624},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":459},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":443},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":419},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Cons